#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244070 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124489 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "073042ee-d96d-5872-9681-ddcea43a2d37" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Sets of Vectors in a Matrix Vector Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "308a8ed8-8d44-58b9-aaf1-e33979b6d166" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447932} {:answer/id #uuid "958d6e8a-0fac-5601-9779-a8576a42bcc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "308a8ed8-8d44-58b9-aaf1-e33979b6d166" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447932} :answer-field/id #uuid "d732d5d9-f30e-58c7-83a6-3a8d5892d490" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449006} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cd517ee-8673-500f-afa1-eacdcea416ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447934} {:answer/id #uuid "de76f5a0-c339-57f4-bd81-cfd33d090fff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447935}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de76f5a0-c339-57f4-bd81-cfd33d090fff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447935} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c241e3e-a093-58cd-84f4-dc30c59df211" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449007} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b3623b7-9257-514c-a794-738ea4d98ff3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447936} {:answer/id #uuid "93d8002f-68fd-5bd3-bbf4-5523386f1e7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447937}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b3623b7-9257-514c-a794-738ea4d98ff3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447936} :answer-field/id #uuid "034309db-1b50-5a7d-a770-c53c3d718529" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f3a9f3d6-7047-5a33-b81e-ab3641786305" :question/math-academy-id "q-107536" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- **II.** $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- **III.** ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+(-1)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere some coefficients are nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]=1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+5\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244093 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124490 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca956d3e-37a8-5f0f-a062-97d0567d54e6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Bases of Abstract Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "605acb7f-0c8c-50ed-ab0c-0e1db5013edb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447938} {:answer/id #uuid "657b255a-5461-5921-9c09-1dfc9a49f297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "605acb7f-0c8c-50ed-ab0c-0e1db5013edb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447938} :answer-field/id #uuid "201145fb-e785-5042-9cad-9304f8fd4c49" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449009} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce65d0a2-bcf1-5386-80c6-bae2988afc8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447940} {:answer/id #uuid "87304bb0-dbab-5188-af76-2353aaf3b331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce65d0a2-bcf1-5386-80c6-bae2988afc8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447940} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec75e391-e956-5ba8-93b0-974ba31d1b28" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449010} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fdb24fab-b924-5594-acf5-3145248ec006" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447942} {:answer/id #uuid "a5f09d35-1adc-5fc8-b63a-eba17838500d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5f09d35-1adc-5fc8-b63a-eba17838500d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447943} :answer-field/id #uuid "c92d836a-ca99-5b03-92e4-d7ddb6e6d591" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d745992-cf32-533c-af78-280d0db594aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-107587" :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}\\}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{c}_{1}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n\n- **II.** $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n\n- **III.** $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+{c}_{3}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ and $C$ are bases, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ and $C$ are maximally linearly independent sets in ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively, and\n\n- $B$ spans ${R}^{2},$ while $C$ spans ${R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{c}_{1}\\}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the set contains a vector from another vector space.\n\n- Statement II is false. The set $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{2}$ because the set contains vectors from another vector space.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}+{x}_{3}({c}_{1}+{c}_{3}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1}+{x}_{3}){c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}+{x}_{3}{c}_{3} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+{c}_{3}\\}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $C$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+{c}_{3}\\}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}+{x}_{3}{c}_{3}=({x}_{1}-{x}_{3}){c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}+{x}_{3}({c}_{1}+{c}_{3})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244094 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124490 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca956d3e-37a8-5f0f-a062-97d0567d54e6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Bases of Abstract Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "770d2c3d-fab1-5534-b9f7-4513807d6490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447944} {:answer/id #uuid "c60c30e7-c8d1-56da-a7b1-dc52f59da093" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "770d2c3d-fab1-5534-b9f7-4513807d6490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447944} :answer-field/id #uuid "890929b2-27b3-5456-a5f9-abd2dd6daf0a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449012} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e52ae039-5ca2-5c93-a689-46d8b5dd4bea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447946} {:answer/id #uuid "08849927-52fb-5b91-8112-4419f09049f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447947}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08849927-52fb-5b91-8112-4419f09049f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447947} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7efffcf-b71e-5e32-915d-99527df6ddf3" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449013} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01c41aa3-6b8b-5b83-92bb-a4e15d730974" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447948} {:answer/id #uuid "7abed29e-4e66-5c0a-a1f1-fc5b1148032b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01c41aa3-6b8b-5b83-92bb-a4e15d730974" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447948} :answer-field/id #uuid "c61da28f-b277-5691-8f60-64afce3eb353" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449014}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "013a3d9d-c3ab-5357-a425-3057635e56b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-107588" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3},x}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$\n- **II.** ${{b}_{1},2{b}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n- **III.** ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},2{c}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ and $C$ are bases, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ and $C$ are maximally linearly independent sets in ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively, and\n\n- $B$ spans ${R}^{2},$ while $C$ spans ${R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$, any vector of ${R}^{3},$ including the given vector $x,$ can be written as a linear combination of ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}.$ This means that ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3},x}$ is linearly dependent and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}({b}_{1})+{x}_{2}(2{b}_{2}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+(2{x}_{2}){b}_{2} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, ${{b}_{1},2{b}_{2}}$ spans ${R}^{2}$ since any linear combination of $B$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${{b}_{1},2{b}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}={x}_{1}{b}_{1}+\\frac{{x}_{2}}{2}(2{b}_{2})\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. The set ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},2{c}_{2}}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the third vector is a linear combination of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ This means that the set is linearly dependent and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244115 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105769 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32467c2c-5605-50e9-b961-a29535a0effb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ccffe726-ee51-5e38-82f6-e00cb9bc6c33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447950} {:answer/id #uuid "10830c4b-8858-530c-855b-cb0eb6404dbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccffe726-ee51-5e38-82f6-e00cb9bc6c33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447950} :answer-field/id #uuid "082071d0-4be9-5965-b725-b0852043bd08" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449015} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c189d83a-2ee8-541b-acb8-4506740a7835" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447952} {:answer/id #uuid "79efbd22-8415-5e36-8323-929f43ac9b5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79efbd22-8415-5e36-8323-929f43ac9b5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447953} :answer-field/id #uuid "05f42b2e-8f6e-5810-a2ac-aac6067c0c01" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449016} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75a10b66-5a8f-5871-9339-219b0e3e74d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447954} {:answer/id #uuid "f07c08ea-4ded-5969-9a0d-3e0fe297bfa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75a10b66-5a8f-5871-9339-219b0e3e74d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447954} :answer-field/id #uuid "cefce898-b060-52ac-83be-8cd47e2e1931" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5fa81e71-e4f5-5405-bfdd-41cd7387b276" :question/math-academy-id "q-108044" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1.$\n\n- **II.** $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3.$\n\n- **III.** If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2,$ then $3v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-6.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $Av=v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =1$ (but not $-1$).\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-3v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $Av=-3v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(3v)=3Av=3(-2v)=-2(3v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(3v)=-2(3v),$ and $3v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $3v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-2$ (but not $-6$).\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244251 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04926916-8e08-5401-a57b-3fd333741913" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors Using Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "925f6c83-b4ce-51d4-84e1-5c2b7159a0ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447956} {:answer/id #uuid "e5417304-9283-5c96-a087-38bcbcdff7d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "925f6c83-b4ce-51d4-84e1-5c2b7159a0ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447956} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e8ecf80-42ee-515b-b840-0fca4fd51e91" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449018} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7647f556-78ec-5982-ba1b-8299380ee419" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447958} {:answer/id #uuid "105f3f91-5cf0-546b-909a-6e64deb9e073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447959}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "105f3f91-5cf0-546b-909a-6e64deb9e073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447959} :answer-field/id #uuid "22c8f626-5c0a-5d3b-a89f-bdf10dc6d81a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449019} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab02dd1a-b910-5b43-a6e7-cc297b78f267" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447960} {:answer/id #uuid "44e170f0-370c-5bd1-99f0-160629623843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447961}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44e170f0-370c-5bd1-99f0-160629623843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447961} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a65b626-9c84-56ff-a5d7-5d5514cd8b08" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449020}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2cf2c42d-88e3-50f2-bb3a-16ab087d4389" :question/math-academy-id "q-109236" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A-I$ and $A+4I$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x,$ $y$ and $z$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $w$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$\n\n- **II.** $u$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$\n\n- **III.** $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since\n\n$$\nw\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-I$ maps $w$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $w$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1,$ not $2.$\n\n- Statement II is  true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\nu\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-I=A-1I$ maps $u$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $u$ is a generalzed eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding  to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\ny\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+4I$ maps $y$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ \ncorresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244253 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04926916-8e08-5401-a57b-3fd333741913" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors Using Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ee7911b-0386-59a4-b189-6fa01d839615" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447968} {:answer/id #uuid "07ec7f79-9597-58d0-bfa1-9c22f08bbdaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ee7911b-0386-59a4-b189-6fa01d839615" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447968} :answer-field/id #uuid "adbac5a9-4944-5267-8e94-c9a806bc297c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449024} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60da78ea-51d0-5afa-8267-0088a36ea71c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447970} {:answer/id #uuid "6ccf6993-ba48-5296-8e57-7e8a0304901c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ccf6993-ba48-5296-8e57-7e8a0304901c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447971} :answer-field/id #uuid "7600750c-0adc-5d31-b0cb-3255a6597126" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449025} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a613fe37-9b3b-573a-850b-34ef10e0a59a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447972} {:answer/id #uuid "d262ae77-ddf1-5d10-a0e6-3abac9cc82f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447973}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d262ae77-ddf1-5d10-a0e6-3abac9cc82f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447973} :answer-field/id #uuid "ceb331e8-6394-5130-95ef-be4bbe04bb33" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449026}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3a61bdaa-5171-5017-8ae8-0bac73d29cdb" :question/math-academy-id "q-109244" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A-6I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-6I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-6I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A-6I$ and $A+7I$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x,$ $y$ and $z$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $w$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =6$ of $A$\n\n- **II.** $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7$ of $A$\n\n- **III.** $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since\n\n$$\nw\\overset{A-6I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-6I$ maps $w$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $w$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1,$ not $3.$\n\n- Statement II is  true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\nx\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+7I$ maps $x$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $x$ is a generalzed eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding  to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\ny\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+7I$ maps $y$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ \ncorresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7$ of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244262 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04926916-8e08-5401-a57b-3fd333741913" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors Using Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "504a18a5-345e-5f33-a5e3-d4ed223fe6d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447974} {:answer/id #uuid "7e40f8bc-be18-53e3-a20f-b84519222b50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447975}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "504a18a5-345e-5f33-a5e3-d4ed223fe6d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447974} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cbfdcfa-02fc-555b-9aa6-7219bdf49d93" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449027} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bfbb9df-12f6-5fde-aa60-33f18308ec49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447976} {:answer/id #uuid "936804af-24f5-53c3-b199-576356af29b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "936804af-24f5-53c3-b199-576356af29b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447977} :answer-field/id #uuid "943da6cf-e34c-5a3c-9ea2-9fc3a4e7d8ae" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449028} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4998305c-1061-57e9-b398-d8cddd576239" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447978} {:answer/id #uuid "07886261-7fa6-56f0-ac70-582505b6c701" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447979}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4998305c-1061-57e9-b398-d8cddd576239" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447978} :answer-field/id #uuid "e95becf4-5560-512b-a495-33b9eabed8ad" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1122434b-dbdd-5a52-98a7-34990f65e51f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109273" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A+8I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A+8I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+8I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A+8I$ and $A$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x$ and $y$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $v$ is a generalized  eigenvector of rank $1$  corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-8$ of $A$\n- **II.** $u$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-8$ of $A$\n- **III.** $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I  is false. Since\n\n$$\nv\\overset{A+8I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+8I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+8I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ not $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\nu\\overset{A+8I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A+8I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+8I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+8I$ maps $u$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $u$ is an generalized eigenvector of rank $3$  corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-8$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since\n\n$$\ny\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A$ maps $y$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ (a.k.a. an eigenvector), not $2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244274 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04926916-8e08-5401-a57b-3fd333741913" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors Using Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52b6901a-7f30-59c2-8f9b-bb1f7bb6392b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447980} {:answer/id #uuid "285060bb-d9e4-5605-bb72-908693204789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447981}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52b6901a-7f30-59c2-8f9b-bb1f7bb6392b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447980} :answer-field/id #uuid "e998d3b0-8cdc-52cd-bb0a-0108ab2c7a5b" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449030} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "548ab684-d281-57c6-a4e4-57d9d4e05ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447982} {:answer/id #uuid "deda5004-ebb2-5168-93c6-97541b774775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447983}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "548ab684-d281-57c6-a4e4-57d9d4e05ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447982} :answer-field/id #uuid "16f030cd-9193-5f68-86d9-ffa18e3a1d83" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449031} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d46a9e24-8cca-5d59-a87f-71b1f6c17bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447984} {:answer/id #uuid "2ef99329-6999-5f7f-bf00-37bb8b85761b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ef99329-6999-5f7f-bf00-37bb8b85761b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447985} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a8c9fe0-1804-511a-878d-7808f422bf08" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449032}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "65e6b2ee-a472-5db0-bf96-384d33845a1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-109360" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A+9I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A+9I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+9I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A+9I$ and $A-I$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x,$ $y$ and $z$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-9$ of $A$\n\n- **II.** $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$\n\n- **III.** $z$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false.  Since \n\n$$\nv\\overset{A+9I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+9I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\n and $v\\ne 0,$ \ni.e. $A+9I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ not $1.$\n\n- Statement II is  false. Since \n\n$$\nx\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\n \ni.e. $A-I$ maps $x$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3,$ not $2.$\n\n- Statement III is true.  Indeed, since \n\n$$\nz\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-1I$ maps $z$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $w$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244291 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152225 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04926916-8e08-5401-a57b-3fd333741913" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors Using Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "488d514c-7856-5bb1-9f27-61a06e12356e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447986} {:answer/id #uuid "2a601700-14b1-5b37-bc21-e9cb82917252" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447987}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "488d514c-7856-5bb1-9f27-61a06e12356e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447986} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd010aa9-624f-5401-9ca7-3ba049f13042" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449033} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "268e1ff9-ffef-534e-9702-84cc79d956ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447988} {:answer/id #uuid "f8ecc2bf-cf19-5dcd-8dc0-e365d5028bf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447989}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "268e1ff9-ffef-534e-9702-84cc79d956ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447988} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ad696ab-44e1-56ba-b3ee-b3265cbb73ea" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449034} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27b058b5-afc0-5cd6-ae4d-bd0a5bc33886" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447990} {:answer/id #uuid "69ae4757-e4e5-5d91-83ff-acbbd8852af9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69ae4757-e4e5-5d91-83ff-acbbd8852af9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447991} :answer-field/id #uuid "43c7aab5-a6e9-5835-b3b5-7835e5599b92" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1c683813-90e6-5581-9ed4-32de3ac9d89f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109431" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\overset{A+20I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+20I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A-15I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A-15I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A+20I$ and $A-15I$ on the nonzero vectors $v,w,x$ and $y$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-20$ of $A$\n- **II.** $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =15$ of $A$\n- **III.** $w$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-20$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since \n\n$$\nv\\overset{A+20I\\,}{\\to }w,\n$$\n\nand $w\\ne 0,$  the vector $v$ is  **not** a eigenvector corresponding  to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-20$  of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since\n\n$$\nx\\overset{A-15I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A-15I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\n \ni.e. $A-15I$ maps $x$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ not $3.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\nw\\overset{A+20I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+20I$ maps $w$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $w$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ (a.k.a. an eigenvector) corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-20$ of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247218 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092329 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a0cc5ea-7f70-5c5f-841e-01a6813da8c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Operations on Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4297f89-fd28-59d0-8a01-1af1a32b248a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447992} {:answer/id #uuid "66b18ac9-a067-5e9e-bebd-cda7eba3504b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66b18ac9-a067-5e9e-bebd-cda7eba3504b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447993} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6e83c0a-ba45-5a06-aff2-f4a09ceb7761" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449036} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "504aaa69-9711-59c3-a3b5-ab54642bafb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447994} {:answer/id #uuid "9524d92b-ef29-5e55-9a7b-fe70c47c1538" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447995}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "504aaa69-9711-59c3-a3b5-ab54642bafb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447994} :answer-field/id #uuid "062e4517-2a59-51d0-9e87-12b76ecd0afe" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449037} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f9d306f-8ce1-56ae-a6c6-235700e213ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447996} {:answer/id #uuid "17477713-8cd0-55aa-a429-b3b2a613cd40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f9d306f-8ce1-56ae-a6c6-235700e213ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447996} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fb69639-775d-5bc7-808b-b95d936c7455" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bc384b99-060e-5785-8885-6518d3e3cae1" :question/math-academy-id "q-111385" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2$ and $4,$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $P+Q$ is always a polynomial\n- **II.** $P-Q$ is sometimes a polynomial of degree $3$\n- **III.** $\\frac{P}{Q}$ is sometimes a polynomial" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Polynomials are closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must contain ${x}^{2}$ and ${x}^{4},$ respectively. Since there is no ${x}^{4}$ term in $P$ that could cancel the ${x}^{4}$ term in $Q,$ the leading term in $P-Q$  still contains ${x}^{4}.$  Therefore, the degree of $P-Q$ must always be $4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For the cases where $\\frac{P}{Q}$ is a polynomial, we need that the degree of $P$ should be greater or equal to the degree of $Q,$ which is not the case.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247219 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092329 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a0cc5ea-7f70-5c5f-841e-01a6813da8c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Operations on Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cc8055b-41ba-5afd-a4b7-8f5ae98c1bbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447998} {:answer/id #uuid "713dda88-c35d-5b62-ba3e-3a7c5c9158ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "713dda88-c35d-5b62-ba3e-3a7c5c9158ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447999} :answer-field/id #uuid "0158263e-6b5a-5d03-b569-19c98b4ea4f9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449039} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a60c243-874b-5e7d-a9de-205150ddf19d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448000} {:answer/id #uuid "bd3c6957-3df1-556b-a8a4-d04a20eb8b38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd3c6957-3df1-556b-a8a4-d04a20eb8b38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448001} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f10c59e-096d-53c3-b35b-f56e4b0a76cc" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449040} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da3dc2a6-e40a-5ffd-bd17-d85ee2cefe2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448002} {:answer/id #uuid "9401d805-a8db-578d-8812-e1292cd7e0f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da3dc2a6-e40a-5ffd-bd17-d85ee2cefe2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448002} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4b462fe-7ba1-5816-9b5d-0503d1cb0a7d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5d80a52b-66a2-53ce-b5ad-ecebb8222def" :question/math-academy-id "q-111390" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $P\\cdot Q$ is a polynomial of degree $4$\n- **II.** $P-Q$ is sometimes a polynomial of degree $1$\n- **III.** $P+Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $2$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must both contain ${x}^{2}.$ Therefore, the highest-degree term in $P\\cdot Q$ must contain ${x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}={x}^{2+2}={x}^{4},$ meaning that $P\\cdot Q$ has degree $4.$\n\n- Statement II is true. For example, suppose that $P={x}^{2}+x$ and $Q={x}^{2}.$ Then, $P-Q={x}^{2}+x-{x}^{2}=x,$ which is a polynomial of degree $1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, suppose $P={x}^{2}$ and $Q=-{x}^{2}.$ Then, $P+Q={x}^{2}+(-{x}^{2})=0,$ which is not a polynomial of degree $2.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247254 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e60bc4-ae61-5c39-ad80-14e0cdab14da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97c4aa29-25a2-5420-aaaa-474634177ffc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448004} {:answer/id #uuid "6239020d-aaf4-5fe4-a820-c620ab71e99a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448005}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97c4aa29-25a2-5420-aaaa-474634177ffc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448004} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef24bf57-2f01-5432-9ac4-0d48397c5756" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449042} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08f95c68-b61f-5133-b49d-6adac9b4640c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448006} {:answer/id #uuid "1be585ce-f404-5d77-bc35-25a343b2c635" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448007}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08f95c68-b61f-5133-b49d-6adac9b4640c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448006} :answer-field/id #uuid "b996260e-b2d2-5f6f-a512-3f34a15eb2bf" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449043} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d65f7c26-d93c-564a-b50c-12755d1b153e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448008} {:answer/id #uuid "758f8f50-5658-5025-8870-c168848da070" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "758f8f50-5658-5025-8870-c168848da070" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448009} :answer-field/id #uuid "baeae7a3-137e-50fe-b6e4-63f32aa365c4" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eeced2e5-384e-5308-b21f-d88eb6c697e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-112252" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A$\n- **II.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A$\n- **III.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A-4I:$\n\n$$\nA-4I=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-4I:$\n\n$$\n(A-4I)v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-4I:$\n\n$$\n(A-4I)w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A-4I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-4I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247409 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105709 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "33965139-0c6a-50fc-993a-e2acd6760212" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a32a048d-f2cb-54f2-9d1c-d5c3468e04c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448010} {:answer/id #uuid "b7098d04-def7-5d1a-80e5-e99f3272049a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a32a048d-f2cb-54f2-9d1c-d5c3468e04c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448010} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5bb018e-2a62-5571-a516-050637012e4e" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449045} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa8115eb-6e91-5df7-bcac-d93c78c8f383" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448012} {:answer/id #uuid "cb9cca76-a57f-55b6-ac71-e3048ca44dfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448013}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb9cca76-a57f-55b6-ac71-e3048ca44dfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448013} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b656c7d-4a13-5813-aa66-e861fb5ded55" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449046} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ebebaf5-a6d7-594e-b367-839ab189ccb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448014} {:answer/id #uuid "b9ef1912-45da-5352-b875-d5361ff285e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9ef1912-45da-5352-b875-d5361ff285e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448015} :answer-field/id #uuid "c08fa681-305e-5e38-af33-ece01598330e" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4af7007e-9d89-5ab4-8e7e-891076eaef01" :question/math-academy-id "q-113756" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+4,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ is a local maximum\n- **II.** $x=0$ is a local maximum\n- **III.** $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ is a local minimum" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =6{x}^{2}-2x \\\\  & =2x(3x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(3x-1)\\,⟹\\,x=0,\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,\\frac{1}{4},1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =2(-1)(3(-1)-1)=8>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-\\frac{1}{4}) & =2(\\frac{1}{4})[3(\\frac{1}{4})-1]=-\\frac{1}{8}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =2(1)(3(1)-1)=4>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\frac{1}{3})$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $(\\frac{1}{3},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=\\frac{1}{3},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ is a local minimum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247410 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105709 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "33965139-0c6a-50fc-993a-e2acd6760212" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4141d585-9a26-5c05-b314-a502e6c516a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448016} {:answer/id #uuid "505582ef-1aef-57a8-9ce7-4d38dfdd5704" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448017}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "505582ef-1aef-57a8-9ce7-4d38dfdd5704" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448017} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef2bc770-f435-5bd8-99a3-74a5171df35e" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449048} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb11f7ca-fbfa-5dbc-ba7c-6491a4d66a8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448018} {:answer/id #uuid "b5658152-f15d-5505-8c77-5717f8e0daf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448019}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5658152-f15d-5505-8c77-5717f8e0daf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448019} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b638333-bcb5-5fb3-b3f9-bbefccae69b1" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449049} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0702bcf1-cf08-5269-b943-98a8b5eab715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448020} {:answer/id #uuid "c9fc66a4-8679-5e2c-b09f-62638a0f8fc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0702bcf1-cf08-5269-b943-98a8b5eab715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448020} :answer-field/id #uuid "85c5cf9c-9a56-5bd8-9808-1b05b4dbfab2" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "04886f18-ebcf-50be-b62d-6d531bb447d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-113758" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x=-\\frac{5}{3}$ is a local maximum\n- **II.** $x=0$ is a local minimum\n- **III.** $x=\\frac{5}{3}$ is a local minimum" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =6{x}^{2}+10x \\\\  & =2x(3x+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(3x+5)\\,⟹\\,x=0,-\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0$ and $x=-\\frac{5}{3}.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-2,-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & =2(-2)(3(-2)+5)=4>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =2(-1)(3(-1)+5)=-4<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =2(1)(3(1)+5)=16>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{3})$ | $-\\frac{5}{3}$ | $(-\\frac{5}{3},0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-2$ |  | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-\\frac{5}{3},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-\\frac{5}{3}$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local minimum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250217 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184151 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals of Concavity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2bbc1d9-5734-59e8-8fc8-7b6c674c67b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448022} {:answer/id #uuid "3b3ae338-b812-5c8a-824a-e79b401f8d2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2bbc1d9-5734-59e8-8fc8-7b6c674c67b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448022} :answer-field/id #uuid "752de745-0621-5409-947c-19362f0dd84f" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449051} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42c0206e-5daa-5aab-bd99-aa60e11d2422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448024} {:answer/id #uuid "d3d5652c-9bc4-593a-82a1-a0d3968f2e46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3d5652c-9bc4-593a-82a1-a0d3968f2e46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448025} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ae3e41d-7d96-5607-aa7e-628fc697cf0e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449052} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b853d92c-2288-5bf6-b37e-e337642d9dad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448026} {:answer/id #uuid "f9456f02-4552-5b67-8c10-0c7758cb593d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b853d92c-2288-5bf6-b37e-e337642d9dad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448026} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c28a799-a132-5f6e-a3b8-d8bf0c6a857b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aab077f8-99df-5fb1-8e2c-0476334114c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-114270" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x+5}{{e}^{x}}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-1,1)$\n- **II.** $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,-1)$\n- **III.** $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-({x}^{2}+4x+5){e}^{-x}+(2x+4){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2x+1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =-(x+1)^{2}{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(x+1)^{2}{e}^{-x}-2(x+1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =(x-1)(x+1){e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)(x+1){e}^{-x} & >0 \\\\ (x-1)(x+1) & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=(x+1)(x-1).$\n\n![](images/09/09b4b237ec87a63e6de47eea5ed9156febbed0271dd0688c0be1c22a62c50c4e.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $(x+1)(x-1)>0$ on  $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(1,\\infty ).$  So the function is concave up on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $(x+1)(x-1)<0,$ which has the solution $(-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250220 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184151 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intervals of Concavity of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c61788c-aa38-569e-b064-a62aa1fafa38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448028} {:answer/id #uuid "962a8718-7882-50c5-b220-0741c4360d8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "962a8718-7882-50c5-b220-0741c4360d8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448029} :answer-field/id #uuid "10778036-82f9-5f0b-9feb-b869e392a286" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449054} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00fe543c-c4a0-5406-80b2-7ad2f17bf1d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448030} {:answer/id #uuid "d977bd44-f4ea-59f9-ba62-6f8c0a48c249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448031}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00fe543c-c4a0-5406-80b2-7ad2f17bf1d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448030} :answer-field/id #uuid "3428e20e-db55-5b2c-bf82-9ce446c21c80" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449055} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4660e71-9cac-5faf-999e-f585c344a5dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448032} {:answer/id #uuid "fa091b57-a8d7-575e-9b8b-b0eb7da3ee75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448033}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa091b57-a8d7-575e-9b8b-b0eb7da3ee75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448033} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa1c36a7-587d-5b7e-874c-0f0edb11faa8" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9e263520-c83b-578e-886f-bcaf93059761" :question/math-academy-id "q-114273" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}(5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x),$ defined on $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f(x)$ is concave up on $(0,e)$\n- **II.** $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,e)$\n- **III.** $f(x)$ is concave down on $(e,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2x(5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{x} \\\\  & =2x(5-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-2x \\\\  & =2x(4-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2(4-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-2x\\cdot \\frac{2}{x} \\\\  & =8-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-4 \\\\  & =4(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) & >0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & <1 \\\\ x & <e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave up on  $(0,e).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $4(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)<0,$ which has the solution $(e,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250261 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064165 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae256269-0bec-5d57-ac4a-eb0b07926f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points and Then Classifying Them Using the Second Derivative Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39a4d35c-0841-541b-b560-2c04eab07774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448034} {:answer/id #uuid "576ed81d-346d-54a5-bdcd-390cabb1b99b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448035}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "576ed81d-346d-54a5-bdcd-390cabb1b99b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448035} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6a7afcf-d257-585a-8030-5f74873574f6" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449057} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26b78632-1e83-53ea-8dec-39f48d7a699d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448036} {:answer/id #uuid "25222a5b-fda0-5cb7-8372-30db249ebf36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448037}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25222a5b-fda0-5cb7-8372-30db249ebf36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448037} :answer-field/id #uuid "4add0ed0-b880-509f-898a-08124b4ba8d3" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449058} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0154929c-b7d9-56cf-8571-78bb55fecdcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448038} {:answer/id #uuid "e329db6c-2dcf-58dd-aa26-72c5775c72f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0154929c-b7d9-56cf-8571-78bb55fecdcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448038} :answer-field/id #uuid "61f7fb03-5a8f-5375-a386-673f2d505b72" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449059} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80d2d71a-65f2-56a2-b3b6-ec354b205e02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448040} {:answer/id #uuid "1fa60caf-ba86-5af1-a00b-444fcc5dc068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fa60caf-ba86-5af1-a00b-444fcc5dc068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448041} :answer-field/id #uuid "95423d4a-b210-5868-b668-c08c1ff0e483" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b90d338d-3db1-5cee-ac01-ae8e8b265add" :question/math-academy-id "q-114415" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=(1-x+{x}^{2}){e}^{x},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=0$\n- **II.** ${f}^{″}(0)>0$\n- **III.** $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=1$\n- **IV.** $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-1$" :question/worked-solution "We need to identify the critical points. Then, we need to classify them using the second derivative test.\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=(1-x+{x}^{2}){e}^{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=(1-x+{x}^{2})\\cdot {e}^{x}+(-1+2x){e}^{x}=({x}^{2}+x){e}^{x},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+x){e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ x(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=-1,0.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the critical points. Computing ${f}^{″}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=({x}^{2}+x)\\cdot {e}^{x}+(2x+1){e}^{x}=({x}^{2}+3x+1){e}^{x},\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{″}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1) & =-{e}^{-1}<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(0) & =1>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that:\n\n- Statements I and II are true. The second derivative is positive at $x=0,$ so there is a relative minimum of $f(x).$\n\n- Statements III is false. $x=1$ is not a critical point.\n\n- Statement IV is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253256 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087700 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a75c67e5-3469-5730-9181-54df4ec6d5b2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Inverse of a Larger Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "373c885e-aef8-50b9-911a-ec047f2d5681" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448042} {:answer/id #uuid "20c8ff2a-dc3c-5659-91b9-08202181b0c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448043}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "373c885e-aef8-50b9-911a-ec047f2d5681" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448042} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed3fa0be-e63d-5195-b281-fe8450f3750c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449061} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "015547b8-bd38-530c-8e48-88bc8e2e2e09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448044} {:answer/id #uuid "1e7fb983-a1ee-5142-94cd-263cd7c41eba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448045}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e7fb983-a1ee-5142-94cd-263cd7c41eba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448045} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5993f1a-def4-558a-814a-d16d4117184e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449062} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40c83f9f-5824-5aed-81dd-43478e72282d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448046} {:answer/id #uuid "95a699cb-8161-554e-9a4d-c4e507a37e99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40c83f9f-5824-5aed-81dd-43478e72282d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448046} :answer-field/id #uuid "d65054a8-c89c-52ab-a032-831b9f5b6b89" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9db68f61-a1ba-5fec-8a45-32913742b631" :question/math-academy-id "q-11634" :question/problem "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,C=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $AB=BA={I}_{3}$\n- **II.** $AC=CA={I}_{3}$\n- **III.** $B={A}^{-1}$" :question/worked-solution "- First, we check whether $AB=BA={I}_{3}.$ Computing $AB$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne {I}_{3}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is false. As a result, $B\\ne {A}^{-1}$ and statement III is false too.\n\n- Now, we check whether $AC=CA={I}_{3}.$ Computing $AC$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{3}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $CA$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CA & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{3}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is true. \nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253257 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087700 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a75c67e5-3469-5730-9181-54df4ec6d5b2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Inverse of a Larger Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "571118f7-834d-565e-8ce1-6917716176bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448048} {:answer/id #uuid "feda0f47-91fe-5b24-a64c-d9d72c65d7be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "feda0f47-91fe-5b24-a64c-d9d72c65d7be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448049} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f2afe7a-b39e-52d9-b782-4c29afe645ed" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449064} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4daa118-2951-524a-a8a5-a07a11973d0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448050} {:answer/id #uuid "02b9525c-5aa7-5637-9eb7-f2af65078057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448051}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4daa118-2951-524a-a8a5-a07a11973d0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448050} :answer-field/id #uuid "72a46244-d72a-5634-be81-4125503e6ad9" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449065} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f8c239a-1d74-5503-87f3-5a2380f75c99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448052} {:answer/id #uuid "c8086890-3a86-5a1b-876b-fba7bced727e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448053}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f8c239a-1d74-5503-87f3-5a2380f75c99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448052} :answer-field/id #uuid "55292d74-822b-5992-b5bf-236b8ac95ebf" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3a987f21-4e16-5f44-b8f8-8e69cf93fc24" :question/math-academy-id "q-11636" :question/problem "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 7 & -17\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 7 & -17\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $AC=CA={I}_{3}$\n- **II.** $AD=DA={I}_{3}$\n- **III.** $D={A}^{-1}$" :question/worked-solution "- First, we check whether $AC=CA={I}_{3}.$ Computing $AC$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 7 & -17\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{3}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $CA$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CA & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 7 & -17\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{3}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true.\n\n- Now, we check whether $AD=DA={I}_{3}.$ Computing $AD$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AD & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 7 & -17\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & 7 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne {I}_{3}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false. As a result, $D\\ne {A}^{-1}$ and statement III is false too.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253258 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087699 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b9ef325-2358-5041-8a8d-b459e8ed9f09" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4efcf39-ef5c-51be-9197-fd336dcc21cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448054} {:answer/id #uuid "46eb2630-2587-530d-ae7f-6568bb2b9247" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46eb2630-2587-530d-ae7f-6568bb2b9247" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448055} :answer-field/id #uuid "66536e33-097f-5b8e-aabe-387b94e6dcdf" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449067} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd936360-4ab5-54cc-85f8-f730e2f97b11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448056} {:answer/id #uuid "59684c94-4157-5480-bbf9-8f31eab89e38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448057}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd936360-4ab5-54cc-85f8-f730e2f97b11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448056} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa70be3f-3e90-53c6-b327-0f504c4a38ba" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449068} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c207fb4-dbd6-51a8-b240-b57b53b19e36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448058} {:answer/id #uuid "0e460479-68f8-53e2-8de7-0af880f9bb8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448059}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e460479-68f8-53e2-8de7-0af880f9bb8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448059} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0967798-fafd-515e-b212-b3143643fbf1" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "297364cd-4444-5a41-ba36-21b226267ecd" :question/math-academy-id "q-11637" :question/problem "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,E=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $CD=DC={I}_{2}$\n\n- **II.** $DE=ED={I}_{2}$\n\n- **III.** $D={C}^{-1}$" :question/worked-solution "- First, we check whether $CD=DC={I}_{2}.$ Computing $CD$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CD & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $DC$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}DC & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true. As a result, $D={C}^{-1}$ and statement III is true too.\n\n- Now, we check whether $DE=ED={I}_{2}.$ Computing $DE$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}DE & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ -2 & ∗\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne {I}_{2}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253501 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e93fd7f-070f-5359-aabb-cba5cc275015" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Convergent or Divergent Integrals With Unbounded Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb5142f6-3668-5c25-bd3b-ad8d86c32b81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448060} {:answer/id #uuid "7f997281-b88c-5dd5-bfb8-151c4ca4f3b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb5142f6-3668-5c25-bd3b-ad8d86c32b81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448060} :answer-field/id #uuid "38c97076-8571-5bd9-b9cc-05ead189c06a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449070} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83a8cf32-8c05-57f0-b07c-8619e74a5e4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448062} {:answer/id #uuid "2190c7c9-638d-5cac-b33b-bf19444c205d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2190c7c9-638d-5cac-b33b-bf19444c205d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448063} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa4d3171-d27d-5383-84f5-fff37db3a18f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449071} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d086c6fe-ca1b-589b-9bf5-edc298cbf2b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448064} {:answer/id #uuid "4c940020-f387-5c2f-8a62-86f9ec0bd046" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448065}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c940020-f387-5c2f-8a62-86f9ec0bd046" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448065} :answer-field/id #uuid "29dae20b-066b-57e8-839e-ee1a6bc08141" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "260fa3cd-f59d-50c6-a56e-8cfbd29393f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-118755" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is convergent\n\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent\n\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution.  Let $u={x}^{2}+4$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4))|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to \\infty $, we have that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)\\to \\infty .$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4))|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{2}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to -\\infty $, we have that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)\\to \\infty .$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement III is true. The integral in statement III can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHowever, both integrals on the right-hand side are divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nIn conclusion,  only statements II and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253504 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e93fd7f-070f-5359-aabb-cba5cc275015" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Convergent or Divergent Integrals With Unbounded Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "868fce7e-dc8d-506d-8541-a2b33751a81f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448066} {:answer/id #uuid "93200da6-dc06-5778-8c75-662454876dc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93200da6-dc06-5778-8c75-662454876dc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448067} :answer-field/id #uuid "6008c1c7-0c91-5c4f-b77d-3d2001714e26" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449073} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da1f27e2-e43a-595a-af92-12d42e44e236" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448068} {:answer/id #uuid "c4a72708-a8dd-5f39-8ec8-0aa6abdf7840" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da1f27e2-e43a-595a-af92-12d42e44e236" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448068} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba4664ca-f99b-51de-bc33-708da1a4905c" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449074} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1db0002c-4097-593b-977f-e88306dd33de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448070} {:answer/id #uuid "5820192e-8f09-59ad-9b73-592b6892f4dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448071}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5820192e-8f09-59ad-9b73-592b6892f4dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448071} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc2690eb-e811-51ff-94f1-5c1490a665aa" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449075}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "96df78fc-91b5-5b18-90a4-e309d8513bb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-118761" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is convergent\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution.  Let $u={x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}))|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to \\infty $, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2})$ oscillates between $-1$ and $1.$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}))|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2})) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to -\\infty $, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2})$ oscillates between $-1$ and $1.$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement III is true. The integral in statement III can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHowever, both integrals on the right-hand side are divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253550 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3aa84ae8-6663-5aa1-844b-89090bcce735" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Slope Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55e35797-c4cc-588f-a334-6e167d806dcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448072} {:answer/id #uuid "bf6dc8e7-0a87-5da6-a74f-4c8397d0c042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448073}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf6dc8e7-0a87-5da6-a74f-4c8397d0c042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448073} :answer-field/id #uuid "22f75b47-16b6-5dd6-a760-d4992c5c2524" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449076} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d765046-e075-5330-b681-0d916148575b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448074} {:answer/id #uuid "76d506ba-3407-5dfa-b117-bc384b0cc378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448075}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76d506ba-3407-5dfa-b117-bc384b0cc378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448075} :answer-field/id #uuid "9178f62a-187a-5cd8-94b0-3a0de5cf57e5" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449077} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77619b7f-1d91-56eb-b4d3-056373fa62fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448076} {:answer/id #uuid "817742eb-aa8e-5d46-ab84-dc12f41876a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77619b7f-1d91-56eb-b4d3-056373fa62fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448076} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac315583-34df-5bdd-a20d-a5eaf525c2fe" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19539888-a72b-5a8c-9bb4-399cabb49fb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-119194" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2x+2$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** At every point along the line $x=0,$ the slope field has a positive slope\n- **II.** At every point along the line $x=-1,$ the slope field is horizontal\n- **III.** At every point along the line $x=-2,$ the slope field has a positive slope" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $x,$ the slope does not vary with $y.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider a point of the form $(0,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,y)}=2(0)+2=2>0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(0,y)$ is positive for any $y.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(0,y)$ is equal to $2$ for any $y.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(-1,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,y)}=2(-1)+2=0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(-1,y)$ is horizontal for any $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Consider a point of the form $(-2,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,y)}=2(-2)+2=-2<0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(-2,y)$ is negative for any $y.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(-2,y)$ is equal to $-2$ for any $y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"\n\nWe can use this information to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](images/8d/8d40c405f3f4db66a18e06a5327e86600081a6bc57b502ebed9c94471a0d5857.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253552 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3aa84ae8-6663-5aa1-844b-89090bcce735" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Slope Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b26889df-f672-5f76-93ae-d98cd0173db5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448078} {:answer/id #uuid "9437deb6-cc24-5774-a85c-1c4a52d73000" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9437deb6-cc24-5774-a85c-1c4a52d73000" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448079} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ff68659-ffc5-5fed-89d6-2e1daf181229" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449079} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b94d37f8-23ff-5f0d-9fe1-b5d1972b75ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448080} {:answer/id #uuid "f37c784e-dc31-553e-8974-bc26d2b13ca7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448081}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f37c784e-dc31-553e-8974-bc26d2b13ca7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448081} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ec99642-e984-5d80-aafb-a5b6a95e4ba8" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449080} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d857a423-2edb-5832-8490-16d6fda1aeff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448082} {:answer/id #uuid "b4594e53-bf79-501e-9810-9917d4099c35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d857a423-2edb-5832-8490-16d6fda1aeff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448082} :answer-field/id #uuid "71854520-8289-544a-af3f-146efa12ed30" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449081}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6eca5ac4-aebb-5558-a54f-a273db0d7d0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-119206" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2}-3x$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** At every point along the line $x=2,$  the slope field has a negative slope\n- **II.** At every point along the line $x=0,$  the slope field is horizontal\n- **III.** At every point along the line $y=1,$ the slope of the slope field does not vary" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $x,$ the slope does not vary with $y.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider a point of the form $(2,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,y)}={2}^{2}-3(2)=-2,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(2,y)$ is negative for any $y.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(0,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,y)}={0}^{2}-3(0)=0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope of the slope field at the point $(0,y)$ is horizontal for any $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,1)}={x}^{2}-3x,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope of the slope field at the point $(x,1)$ depends on $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"\n\nWe can use the information given by the true statements to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e2/e2ce8d7df3d0ceee4f8c9dcf368940006f1266a751c2f8e5ea8afdcfb90bf7ac.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253554 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3aa84ae8-6663-5aa1-844b-89090bcce735" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Slope Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de2d8ae0-c0d6-5028-83c2-1907754129e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448084} {:answer/id #uuid "bf134717-da17-5a2a-9007-2d2221e992a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf134717-da17-5a2a-9007-2d2221e992a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448085} :answer-field/id #uuid "901b068f-d1cc-55f3-9847-0c9f43ba1c7f" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449082} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "977772b0-6d10-5f44-acb1-e4ff1e286577" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448086} {:answer/id #uuid "676ee669-c4da-5472-a261-dd93452e6756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "676ee669-c4da-5472-a261-dd93452e6756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448087} :answer-field/id #uuid "530e3e75-0636-5430-906f-95c911d5ecef" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449083} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d2a31a5-7247-5293-a978-37ef33cf6ec5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448088} {:answer/id #uuid "35d8e55d-d1cf-56aa-9069-dba43421e95d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35d8e55d-d1cf-56aa-9069-dba43421e95d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448089} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb4c7df5-21a8-5c7b-809a-f0c5deea3353" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449084}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5e9d7c3e-9e55-5477-a8a9-aed5b3e5371d" :question/math-academy-id "q-119209" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1-{x}^{3}$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** At every point along the line $x=1,$  the slope field is horizontal\n- **II.** At every point along the line $x=0,$  the slope field has a positive slope\n- **III.** At every point along the line $y=2,$ the slope of the slope field varies" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $x,$ the slope does not vary with $y.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider a point of the form $(1,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,y)}=1-{1}^{3}=0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(1,y)$ is horizontal for any $y.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(0,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,y)}=1-{0}^{3}=1>0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope of the slope field at the point $(0,y)$ is positive for any $y.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(0,y)$ is equal to $1$ for any $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,2)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,2)}=1-{x}^{3},\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope of the slope field at the point $(x,2)$ depends on $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"\n\nWe can use the information given by the true statements to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](images/85/85d8f71c4680a56d5722b87fa5f7b2a7623fd2457f540c2cd15cd3bfa29694f3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253568 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172036 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1343e4de-56ee-563c-bdc8-f8c0cdc54f0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Slope Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48fd0ccc-8019-5758-8e77-495fa768b790" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448090} {:answer/id #uuid "fe55d5cc-3414-57dc-afa9-1c945f72f958" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48fd0ccc-8019-5758-8e77-495fa768b790" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448090} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f9bfa14-f07a-57a1-8e61-d35ecbdf56eb" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449085} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b4f0280-3057-5fc5-bdf9-358fb92ea26c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448092} {:answer/id #uuid "3ebcc34f-d79b-5cfb-9967-eab231751295" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ebcc34f-d79b-5cfb-9967-eab231751295" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448093} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9960db6-909e-50a8-aea1-4eb176bb9f4b" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449086} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "def0803c-91ae-5f1c-867c-5a15b7bfeae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448094} {:answer/id #uuid "f09f6bdc-1380-5507-a746-f5a83382df19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "def0803c-91ae-5f1c-867c-5a15b7bfeae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448094} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ec478c5-09f5-5db6-a6bd-44dce48ef43f" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449087}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "95486801-04a3-50a0-ad5b-8d31467cc59e" :question/math-academy-id "q-119324" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-y$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** At every point along the line $y=2,$ the slope field has a positive slope\n- **II.** At every point along the line $y=0,$ the slope field is horizontal\n- **III.** At every point along the line $y=-2,$ the slope field has a negative slope" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,2)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,2)}=-2<0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,2)$ is negative for any $x.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(x,2)$ is equal to $-2$ for any $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,0)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,0)}=-0=0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,0)$ is horizontal for any $x.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,-2)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,-2)}=-(-2)=2>0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,-2)$ is positive for any $x.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(x,-2)$ is equal to $2$ for any $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"\n\nWe can use this information to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](images/8f/8fc0b5be5c9147c29410ff7a082316403302f2bce7ec25c665ab7ccc5bf90b0f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256520 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054632 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Subsets"}] 